==[ GunCode Documentation ]===================================================

This file will hopefully serve as all the technical reference anyone will
ever need to write gun based weapons.  First allow me to explain the
differences between energy guns and "normal" guns:

EGunCode guns use energy to fire beams, plasma, lasers, lightning bolts,
etc.  The energy comes from either internal batteries in the gun, or power
cell objects.  The damage is controlled by the gun, each shot taking a
certain amount of energy to fire.  Different guns will do different damage
and require different amounts of energy per shot, thus, the same power cell
will not always fire the same number of shots in different guns.
  -- See also: CellCode

GunCode guns use bullets (or other projectiles) for ammunition.  Each
projectile is a seperate object and the damage dealt during combat is
controlled primarily by the projectile objects.  The same projectile fired
from multiple guns will tend do similar amounts of damage.  Projectiles can
be stored in the gun itself (ie., a revolver) or in a seperate magazine
(ie., a clip-fed assualt rifle).  The same magazine in different weapons
will always contain the same number of shots.
  -- See also: AmmoCode, MagazineCode

GunCode inherits NewWeaponCode, so all functions like set_proficiency() and
set_handed() are contained in NewWeaponCode.  

Any questions, bug reports, spelling corrections, or enhancement requests
should be addressed to Deathdealer.

=====[ Set Functions ]========================================================

void set_mag_fed( int mf )

     Used to set whether or not the gun is magazine fed or stores ammo itself.
     Default: 1


void set_capacity( int cap )

     Used to set the number of rounds the magazine can hold.  Magazine fed
     guns cannot hold rounds internally and ignore any set capacity.
     Default: 0


void set_ammo_type( string type )

     Used to set the ammunition type of the magazine.  Any ammo that is
     meant to be loaded into the magazine must have the same <type>.
     Default: see set_mag_type


void set_mag_type( string type )

     Used to set the magazine type.  Any gun the magazine is meant to be
     used with must have the same <type>.
     Default: ".22 automatic"

void set_jam_chance( int jam )

     Used to set the extra jam chance when rounds are being fed into the gun.
     Guns with complex feed systems and high rates of fire will tend to jam
     more often.
     Default: 1

     NOTE: jam percentage goes up by 1 for every round fired when the gun is
     used in burst mode, regardless of the set jam_chance.


void set_jam_delay( int delay )

     Used to set combat delay for unjamming a jammed round.  Guns with
     complex feed mechanisms and high rates of fire will tend to have higher
     jam delays.
     Default: 4

void set_op_delay( int delay )

     Used to set the combat delay for loading a round or magazine into the gun.
     Guns with guns with complex feed systems (belt fed, for instance) will
     tend to have higher delays than guns that are easier to operate (a
     standard magazine fed gun).
     Default: 1


void set_queue( int queue )

     Used to set if the gun holds rounds as a stack or a queue.  A normal
     clip for gun is a stack (you push rounds on to it), a belt of ammo for
     a large machine gun is a queue (you add more rounds to the end of it).
     Guns that are magazine fed don't care if they are queue or not, as the
     magazine determines it.
     Default: 0 (stack)


void set_recoil( int recoil )

     Used to set the negative hit bonus per round of ammunition fired during
     a burst.  Guns that fire very powerful rounds or fire them at very high
     velocities will tend to have higher recoil.  Recoil does not matter when
     the gun is fired in single-shot mode.
     Default: 1


void set_burst( int burst )

     Used to set the number of shots the gun can fire during a single combat
     round when in burst mode.  Guns with very high burst rates will tend to
     jam more.
     Default: 1


void set_switchable( int switchable )

     Used to set if a gun can be switched between single-shot and burst modes.
     1 is switchable, 0 is not.
     Default: 0


void set_mode( int mode )

     Used to set starting mode of a gun.  0 is single-shot, 1 is burst-fire.
     Default: 0
     
     NOTE: if burst number is not 2 or greater, mode cannot be set to burst
     fire.


void set_reliability( int reliability )

     Used to set the guns basic reliability.  As the gun gets used on combat,
     it will age and become more likely to jam.  Guns that do large amounts of
     damage should generally have lower reliability ratings.  Reliability can
     be set anywhere from 1 (very very very low) to 200.
     Default: 100

=====[ Query Functions ]======================================================

int query_max_damage( object who )

     Returns calculated max damage, including proficiency and stat bonuses
     for <who>, minus the minimum damage for the gun.


int query_capacity()

     Returns the number of rounds the gun can hold.


string query_ammo_type()

     Returns the type of rounds the gun can hold.


string query_mag_type()

     Returns the magazine type the gun can hold.


int query_jam_chance()

     Returns the extra chance of jamming when feeding rounds into the gun.


int query_op_delay()

     Returns the combat delay for loading a round or a magazine into the gun.


int query_queue()

     Returns if the gun is queue based (1), or stack based (0).

object *query_ammo()

     Returns the ammo objects currently loaded in the magazine.


object query_shot()

     Returns the next shot that will be fired from the gun.


int query_shots()

     Returns the number of shots currently left in the gun.


int query_switchable()

     Returns 1 if the gun can mode switch between single-shot and burst,
     0 if it cannot.


int query_mode()

     Returns 1 if the gun is in burst mode, 0 if in single-shot.


int query_reliability()

     Returns the guns reliability factor.


int query_shots_fired()

     Returns the number of shots fired from the gun.
     NOTE: This is not an absolutely correct number, as it can be
     modified and reduced by maintenance on the gun.  It is only the
     number of shots used when calculating reliability based effects.


int query_value()

     Returns the value of the gun plus the value of any magazine or ammo
     currently contained in it.


int query_weight()

     Returns the weight of the gun, plus the weight of any magazine or ammo
     currently contained in it.


=====[ Utility Functions ]====================================================

varargs void fill_with( string filename, int num )

     Call this function in extra_create() when you want to fill the gun
     with a specific ammunition object (otherwise the gun will start out
     empty).  <num> is an optional argument if you want to specify the number
     of rounds to add, otherwise <num> will default to the guns capacity.
     
     Example:
     
     extra_create() {
       ...
       fill_with( "/obj/weapon/gun/ammo/223_tracer", 3 );
       fill_with( "/obj/weapon/gun/ammo/223" );
       ... }
       
     This would put three tracer rounds in the gun (to be fired last
     unless the queue flag is set), and then fill the gun to capacity
     with normal .223 rounds.


=====[ Optional Functions ]===================================================

int extra_jam( object gun )

     Overload this function if you want something special to happen to the
     magazine in the event of a gun jam.


void extra_hit( object attacker, object target, int roll, int dam )

     Overload this function when you want the gun to do something special
     when a shot fired from it hits the target.


=====[ Functions called in wielder ]==========================================

void reload_gun( object gun )

     This function is called in the wielding monster or player when the gun
     runs out of ammo.  It can be used to make a monster re-load a gun when
     needed, or switch to an alternate weapon, or whatever else you want.


void gun_jammed( object gun )

     This function is called in the the wielding monster or player in the
     event of a gun jam.  It can be used to make the monster un-jam the gun,
     switch to a backup weapon un-jam the gun or whatever else you might want
     to have it do.

